<Layout
	title="Introduction to Trigonometry | The Concepts Adda"
	description="Learn the basics of Trigonometry, right-angled triangles and the fundamental ideas behind trigonometric ratios."
>

	<main class="lesson-page">

		<div class="lesson-container">

			<a href="/trigonometry" class="back-link">
				← Back to Trigonometry
			</a>


			<!-- =========================
			     LESSON HEADER
			========================= -->

			<section class="lesson-header">

				<div class="lesson-number">
					01
				</div>

				<div>

					<div class="lesson-label">
						LESSON 01 · CLASS 10 MATHEMATICS
					</div>

					<h1>
						Introduction to<span> Trigonometry</span>
					</h1>

					<p>
						Understand what trigonometry is and why
						right-angled triangles form its foundation.
					</p>

				</div>

			</section>


			<!-- =========================
			     CONTENT
			========================= -->

			<section class="content-layout">

				<article class="lesson-content">

					<div class="topic-label">
						01 · THE BASICS
					</div>

					<h2>
						What is Trigonometry?
					</h2>

					<p>
						Trigonometry is a branch of Mathematics that
						studies the relationship between the angles
						and sides of a triangle.
					</p>

					<p>
						The word <strong>Trigonometry</strong> comes from
						two Greek words that mean triangle and measurement.
						In Class 10, we mainly study these relationships
						using <strong>right-angled triangles</strong>.
					</p>


					<!-- KEY IDEA -->

					<div class="idea-box">

						<div class="idea-icon">
							✓
						</div>

						<div>
							<strong>Key Idea</strong>

							<p>
								Trigonometry helps us find unknown
								sides or angles of a right-angled
								triangle using known information.
							</p>
						</div>

					</div>


					<!-- =========================
					     RIGHT ANGLED TRIANGLE
					========================= -->

					<div class="topic-label second-label">
						02 · RIGHT-ANGLED TRIANGLE
					</div>

					<h2>
						The foundation of Trigonometry
					</h2>

					<p>
						A right-angled triangle is a triangle in which
						one angle is exactly <strong>90°</strong>.
					</p>


					<div class="triangle-card">

						<div class="triangle-visual">

							<svg
								viewBox="0 0 500 330"
								role="img"
								aria-label="Right angled triangle showing perpendicular, base, hypotenuse and angle theta"
							>

								<!-- TRIANGLE -->

								<polygon
									points="95,265 95,75 405,265"
									fill="#eef4ff"
									stroke="#14213d"
									stroke-width="4"
									stroke-linejoin="round"
								/>


								<!-- RIGHT ANGLE -->

								<polyline
									points="95,235 125,235 125,265"
									fill="none"
									stroke="#2563eb"
									stroke-width="4"
									stroke-linejoin="round"
								/>


								<!-- BASE LABEL -->

								<text
									x="250"
									y="302"
									text-anchor="middle"
									class="svg-label"
								>
									Base
								</text>


								<!-- PERPENDICULAR LABEL -->

								<text
									x="48"
									y="172"
									text-anchor="middle"
									class="svg-label"
									transform="rotate(-90 48 172)"
								>
									Perpendicular
								</text>


								<!-- HYPOTENUSE LABEL -->

								<text
									x="282"
									y="153"
									text-anchor="middle"
									class="svg-label"
									transform="rotate(31.5 282 153)"
								>
									Hypotenuse
								</text>


								<!-- THETA ANGLE -->

								<path
									d="M 365 265 A 40 40 0 0 1 372 242"
									fill="none"
									stroke="#f4c430"
									stroke-width="5"
									stroke-linecap="round"
								/>


								<!-- THETA LABEL -->

								<text
									x="355"
									y="254"
									text-anchor="middle"
									dominant-baseline="middle"
									class="angle-label"
								>
									θ
								</text>

							</svg>

						</div>


						<!-- SIDE INFORMATION -->

						<div class="triangle-info">

							<div class="side-info">

								<div class="side-dot blue-dot"></div>

								<div>
									<strong>Perpendicular</strong>

									<span>
										The side opposite the angle θ.
									</span>
								</div>

							</div>


							<div class="side-info">

								<div class="side-dot yellow-dot"></div>

								<div>
									<strong>Base</strong>

									<span>
										The side adjacent to angle θ,
										other than the hypotenuse.
									</span>
								</div>

							</div>


							<div class="side-info">

								<div class="side-dot navy-dot"></div>

								<div>
									<strong>Hypotenuse</strong>

									<span>
										The side opposite the 90° angle.
										It is always the longest side.
									</span>
								</div>

							</div>

						</div>

					</div>


					<!-- =========================
					     IMPORTANT TERMS
					========================= -->

					<div class="topic-label second-label">
						03 · IMPORTANT TERMS
					</div>

					<h2>
						Know the three sides
					</h2>

					<p>
						Before learning sin, cos and tan, you must
						be able to identify the three sides of a
						right-angled triangle correctly.
					</p>


					<div class="terms-grid">

						<div class="term-card">

							<div class="term-number">
								01
							</div>

							<h3>
								Perpendicular
							</h3>

							<p>
								The side opposite the angle
								we are considering.
							</p>

						</div>


						<div class="term-card">

							<div class="term-number">
								02
							</div>

							<h3>
								Base
							</h3>

							<p>
								The side next to the angle,
								excluding the hypotenuse.
							</p>

						</div>


						<div class="term-card">

							<div class="term-number">
								03
							</div>

							<h3>
								Hypotenuse
							</h3>

							<p>
								The longest side, opposite
								the 90° angle.
							</p>

						</div>

					</div>


					<!-- =========================
					     REMEMBER
					========================= -->

					<div class="remember-card">

						<div class="remember-icon">
							!
						</div>

						<div>

							<span>
								REMEMBER
							</span>

							<strong>
								The hypotenuse is always opposite the 90° angle.
							</strong>

						</div>

					</div>


					<!-- =========================
					     04 · PRACTICE PROBLEMS
					========================= -->

					<div class="topic-label second-label">
						04 · PRACTICE PROBLEMS
					</div>

					<h2>
						Test Your Understanding
					</h2>

					<!-- Example 1 -->
					<div class="example-card">
						<div class="example-header">
							<span class="example-badge">EXAMPLE 1</span>
							<h3>Finding Hypotenuse in a Right Triangle</h3>
						</div>
						<div class="example-solution">
							<p>In a right triangle ΔABC right-angled at B, if AB = 3 cm and BC = 4 cm, find the length of hypotenuse AC.</p>
							<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
							<p>By Pythagoras Theorem: <em>AC² = AB² + BC²</em></p>
							<div class="calc-box">
								AC² = 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25<br/>
								AC = √25 = <strong>5 cm</strong>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<!-- Example 2 -->
					<div class="example-card">
						<div class="example-header">
							<span class="example-badge">EXAMPLE 2</span>
							<h3>Finding Unknown Side Length</h3>
						</div>
						<div class="example-solution">
							<p>In right triangle ΔPQR right-angled at Q, hypotenuse PR = 13 cm and side PQ = 5 cm. Find base QR.</p>
							<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
							<p>By Pythagoras Theorem: <em>PR² = PQ² + QR²</em></p>
							<div class="calc-box">
								13² = 5² + QR²<br/>
								169 = 25 + QR² → QR² = 144<br/>
								QR = √144 = <strong>12 cm</strong>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>


					<!-- =========================
					     SUMMARY
					========================= -->

					<div class="topic-label second-label">
						05 · QUICK SUMMARY
					</div>

					<h2>
						What did we learn?
					</h2>


					<div class="summary-list">

